  "result": "I've implemented `slugify` in `src/strings.js` and added 14 tests to `test/strings.test.js`. `npm test` passes all 16 tests, including the 2 that were already there.\n\nHow it works:\n1. It lowercases the text, then splits accented letters into a base letter plus accent marks and drops the marks. That turns `Tiếng Việt` into `tieng viet` and `Crème Brûlée` into `creme brulee`.\n2. Some letters can't be split that way, so a small table maps them directly: `đ`→`d`, plus `ø`, `ł`, `ß`→`ss`, `æ`→`ae`, `œ`→`oe`, `þ`→`th` and `ð`.\n3. Every run of characters that aren't a-z or 0-9 becomes one hyphen, and hyphens at either end are removed.\n4. It cuts the result to 50 characters, then removes any hyphen left at the end. A cut can therefore give a slug shorter than 50, e.g. 49 letters if character 50 would have been a hyphen.\n\nTwo behaviours you might not assume:\n- **Non-Latin text disappears:** characters like Japanese text or emoji become hyphens, so `'日本'` turns into an empty string.\n- **Non-string input is accepted:** `slugify` converts its input to a string first, so `slugify(null)` returns `'null'` instead of throwing.\n\nThe tests cover Vietnamese text including `Đ`/`đ`, other European accents, separators, digits, input that leaves nothing, a slug of exactly 50 characters, and a cut that lands right after a hyphen. I haven't committed anything.",
